{"key":"apphy2-mcq-002","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"For an ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature (U = (3/2)nRT for a monatomic ideal gas). Isothermal means constant temperature, so ΔU = 0. Choice D is dimensionally inconsistent.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.4 The First Law of Thermodynamics","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8249,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":7865,"cacheReadTokens":384},"outputTokens":574,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":135,"reasoningTokens":439},"totalTokens":8823,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":8249,"completion_tokens":574,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":384,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":7865,"total_tokens":8823,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":384},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":439}},"reasoningTokens":439,"cachedInputTokens":384},"ms":6506,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-004","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"A","working":"By convention, the electric field direction at a point is defined as the direction of the electric force on a positive test charge. The fact pack states that the force on a positive test charge is in the field’s direction. Negative charges would feel an opposite force, neutral objects feel no net electric force, and electrons are negative, not the defining charge.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.3 Electric Fields","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8255,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":7871,"cacheReadTokens":384},"outputTokens":632,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":134,"reasoningTokens":498},"totalTokens":8887,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":8255,"completion_tokens":632,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":384,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":7871,"total_tokens":8887,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":384},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":498}},"reasoningTokens":498,"cachedInputTokens":384},"ms":6863,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-005","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"An electric field points toward decreasing potential and exerts a force on a positive charge in the direction of the field. Moving the positive charge opposite to the field direction requires positive external work against the electric force, increasing the electric potential energy of the charge-field system (ΔU_E = qΔV > 0).","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.4 Electric Potential Energy","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8611,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8611,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":463,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":164,"reasoningTokens":299},"totalTokens":9074,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":299,"promptTokenCount":8611,"candidatesTokenCount":164,"totalTokenCount":9074,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8611}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":164}]},"reasoningTokens":299,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5053,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-006","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"A","working":"Each positive point charge produces an electric field directed away from itself. At the midpoint between two identical charges, the distances to each charge are equal, so the magnitudes of the electric fields are equal. Since the fields point in opposite directions, their vector sum is zero.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.3","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8626,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8626,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":296,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":149,"reasoningTokens":147},"totalTokens":8922,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":147,"promptTokenCount":8626,"candidatesTokenCount":149,"totalTokenCount":8922,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8626}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":149}]},"reasoningTokens":147,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":4669,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-006","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"A","working":"At the midpoint, the left positive charge produces an electric field to the right, and the right positive charge produces an electric field to the left. The charges are identical and at equal distances, so the field magnitudes are equal and the vector sum is zero.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.3 Electric Fields","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8268,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":76,"cacheReadTokens":8192},"outputTokens":617,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":135,"reasoningTokens":482},"totalTokens":8885,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":8268,"completion_tokens":617,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":8192,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":76,"total_tokens":8885,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":8192},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":482}},"reasoningTokens":482,"cachedInputTokens":8192},"ms":6028,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-009","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"Immediately after connection, an uncharged capacitor has zero charge and therefore zero potential difference across its plates (ΔV = Q/C = 0). Consequently, charge flows freely through the branch as if there were zero resistance, meaning the uncharged capacitor behaves like an ideal wire (short circuit).","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"11.8 Resistor-Capacitor (RC) Circuits","required_units":[11],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8624,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8624,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":452,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":166,"reasoningTokens":286},"totalTokens":9076,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":286,"promptTokenCount":8624,"candidatesTokenCount":166,"totalTokenCount":9076,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8624}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":166}]},"reasoningTokens":286,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5305,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-005","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"For a positive charge, electric potential energy is U = qV. A uniform electric field points from higher to lower potential. Moving opposite the field moves the positive charge to a region of higher electric potential, so with q positive, U increases. This also matches doing external work against the electric force.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.4 Electric Potential Energy","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8253,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":61,"cacheReadTokens":8192},"outputTokens":843,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":145,"reasoningTokens":698},"totalTokens":9096,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":8253,"completion_tokens":843,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":8192,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":61,"total_tokens":9096,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":8192},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":698}},"reasoningTokens":698,"cachedInputTokens":8192},"ms":7950,"error":""}
{"key":"apphy2-mcq-025","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"Electric field is related to the spatial rate of change (gradient) of electric potential, not the potential value itself. For example, midway between two equal and opposite charges forming an electric dipole, the electric potential is zero, but the electric field is nonzero (pointing from positive to negative). Thus, the field may be nonzero.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.5","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":8613,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":8613,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":364,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":161,"reasoningTokens":203},"totalTokens":8977,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":203,"promptTokenCount":8613,"candidatesTokenCount":161,"totalTokenCount":8977,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":8613}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":161}]},"reasoningTokens":203,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":4114,"error":""}
